{
  format: 1
  name: surface-view-parameter-inferred-typing
  suite: scenario
  spec: ["#interfaces", "§10.1", "#expressions", "§6.2", "#mutations", "§8.3", "#clients", "§12.1"]
  tags: ["typing", "boundary"]
  note: '''
    §6.2 says `@name` denotes a mutation or view parameter, and §10.1 pins how a
    surface `$view` parameter gets its type: it "is inferred exactly as a
    mutation parameter is (§8.3): every typed use of `@name` — a comparison
    against a typed field, a key-selector position, a typed function argument —
    contributes a constraint, and all uses MUST agree on one type." Here the
    PUBLIC surface `by_state` declares no `$params` at all: the filter
    `t.done == @done` compares `@done` against the `bool` field `tasks.done`, so
    `@done` is inferred `bool`, the surface loads, and its parameter shape is
    part of the external contract exactly as a mutation's is.

    The case asserts the inferred typing is LIVE, not merely permitted to load.
    Attack: having lost the "every `@name` must be declared" gate, a client tries
    to pass a value the inferred type does not admit, hoping an inferred
    parameter is weaker than a declared one and gets coerced or widened to
    `json`. §12.1 step 3 parses arguments "against the inferred or explicit
    shape", so the JSON string "yes" does not decode as `bool` and the request is
    malformed — the inferred contract is enforced at the wire boundary with
    exactly the strength a declared `bool` would have.

    The role-surface path of the same rule is
    `10/role-view-parameter-inferred-typing`; the fail-to-explicit side is
    `10/surface-view-parameter-without-anchor-invalid`.
  '''

  package: {
    $liasse: 1
    $app: "t.viewparams@1.0.0"
    $model: {
      tasks: {
        $key: "id"
        id: "text"
        done: "bool"
      }
      $public: {
        by_state: {
          // No $params: @done is inferred `bool` from `t.done == @done` (§10.1).
          $view: ".tasks[:t | t.done == @done] { id }"
        }
      }
    }
    $data: {
      tasks: {
        t1: { done: false }
        t2: { done: true }
      }
    }
  }

  steps: [
    { connect: "c1" }
    // The inferred `bool` parameter is a real, required input of the contract.
    { watch: "public.by_state", args: { done: true }, on: "c1", id: "w1",
      expect_init: { value: [ { id: "t2" } ] } }
    { watch: "public.by_state", args: { done: false }, on: "c1", id: "w2",
      expect_init: { value: [ { id: "t1" } ] } }
    // A wrongly-typed argument is refused against the INFERRED type, exactly as
    // it would be against a declared one (§12.1 step 3).
    { watch: "public.by_state", args: { done: "yes" }, on: "c1", id: "w3",
      expect: { outcome: rejected, violates: ["#clients", "§12.1"] } }
  ]
}
